Mandahai\">Mandahai</a></div><div class=\"wst-header-forward-arrow\">\u2192</div></div></div><div class=\"wst-header-notes header&#95;notes searchaux wst-unknown-notes\"><style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r14791120\">.mw-parser-output .plainSister{display:block;font-size:93%;line-height:normal;list-style-type:none;list-style-image:none;list-style-position:outside;border:1px solid var(--border-color-interactive--hover,#AAA);float:right;clear:right;margin:0.5ex 0 0.5ex 0.5ex;padding:0;background:var(--background-color-base,#FFFFFF);color:var(--color-base,#202122)}.mw-parser-output .sisicon,.mw-parser-output .dabicon{padding-right:1ex}</style><ul class=\"plainSister\"><li class=\"sisitem\"><span class=\"sisicon\"><span typeof=\"mw:File/Frameless\"><a href=\"/wiki/Special:SiteMatrix\" title=\"Special:SiteMatrix\"><img alt=\"Sister Projects\" src=\"//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Wikimedia-logo.svg/20px-Wikimedia-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikisource.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"18\" height=\"18\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Wikimedia-logo.svg/40px-Wikimedia-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikisource.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x\" data-file-width=\"1024\" data-file-height=\"1024\" /></a></span></span><a href=\"/wiki/Special:SiteMatrix\" title=\"Special:SiteMatrix\">sister projects</a>: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Man_Gui\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"w:Man Gui\">Wikipedia article</a>, <a href=\"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q107367542\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"d:Q107367542\">Wikidata item</a></li></ul></div><div class=\"ws-noexport\" id=\"ws-data\" style=\"speak:none\"><span id=\"ws-article-id\">3646152</span><span id=\"ws-title\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period\">Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period</a>, Volume 1 \u2014 Man Kuei</span><span id=\"ws-editor\">Arthur W. Hummel</span><span id=\"ws-author\">George A. Kennedy</span></div></div>\n<p><span style=\"display:none\" id=\"dynamic&#95;layout&#95;overrider\">Layout 2</span>\n</p>\n<div class=\"prp-pages-output\" lang=\"en\">\n<p><span><span class=\"pagenum ws-pagenum\" id=\"561\" data-page-number=\"561\" data-page-name=\"Page:Eminent Chinese Of The Ch\u2019ing Period - Hummel - 1943 - Vol. 1.pdf/573\" data-page-index=\"573\" data-page-quality=\"3\" title=\"Page:Eminent&#95;Chinese&#95;Of&#95;The&#95;Ch%E2%80%99ing&#95;Period&#95;-&#95;Hummel&#95;-&#95;1943&#95;-&#95;Vol.&#95;1.pdf/573\"><span id=\"pageindex&#95;573\" class=\"pagenum-inner ws-noexport\">&#8203;</span></span></span><b>MAN</b> Kuei \u6eff\u6842, d. 1630, Jan. 29, Ming general, was born a Mongol. He came to China in early life and made his home in Hs\u00fcan-hua, Chihli, where he took great delight in the border warfare that went on about him. He was serving as second captain in a garrison along the Great Wall when the defeat of <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Yang_Hao\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Yang Hao\">Yang Hao</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>] by the Manchus in 1619 awakened China to a realization of the menace from the northeast. Transferred to the Shanhaikuan region, he was rapidly promoted to major and then to lieutenant colonel. In 1622 he attracted the attention of <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Sun_Ch%27%C3%AAng-tsung\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Sun Ch&#39;\u00eang-tsung\">Sun Ch'\u00eang-tsung</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>], was promoted to colonel at headquarters, and in the following year was put in charge of defensive operations at Ning-y\u00fcan. After driving away certain Mongol tribes who were occupying the region of Ta-ling-ho he was promoted to the rank of brigade general. Together with Sun's successor, <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Y%C3%BCan_Ch%27ung-huan\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Y\u00fcan Ch&#39;ung-huan\">Y\u00fcan Ch'ung-huan</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>], he repulsed, early in 1626, the Manchu attack on Ning-y\u00fcan, but was recalled shortly after. Before the end of the year he was stationed at Shanhaikuan. While bringing relief&#32;<span><span class=\"pagenum ws-pagenum\" id=\"562\" data-page-number=\"562\" data-page-name=\"Page:Eminent Chinese Of The Ch\u2019ing Period - Hummel - 1943 - Vol. 1.pdf/574\" data-page-index=\"574\" data-page-quality=\"3\" title=\"Page:Eminent&#95;Chinese&#95;Of&#95;The&#95;Ch%E2%80%99ing&#95;Period&#95;-&#95;Hummel&#95;-&#95;1943&#95;-&#95;Vol.&#95;1.pdf/574\"><span id=\"pageindex&#95;574\" class=\"pagenum-inner ws-noexport\">&#8203;</span></span></span>to Ning-y\u00fcan in July 1627 he was severely wounded. In 1628, under a new emperor, he was recalled from the front and sent to the defense of Tatung, Shansi, against Mongol raids. Late in 1629 he was summoned to the defense of Peking when the Manchus made their first effective thrust toward the capital. After Y\u00fcan Ch'ung-huan was imprisoned and <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Tsu_Ta-shou\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Tsu Ta-shou\">Tsu Ta-shou</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>] fled, Man Kuei was left alone to face the enemy. Despite stubborn resistance he was driven back and killed outside the south wall of Peking. He was canonized as Wu-min \u6b66\u610d.\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431518\">.mw-parser-output .wst-rule{background-color:currentcolor;color:inherit;width:auto;margin:2px auto 2px auto;height:1px;overflow:hidden}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.wst-rule-left{margin-left:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.wst-rule-right{margin-right:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.__uirule{background-color:#a2a9b1;color:inherit;border:0;margin-top:0.2em;margin-bottom:0.2em}</style><hr class=\"wst-rule wst-rule-left\" style=\"color:inherit;width:40%;\" />\n<p>[M.1/171/7a; M.3/234/6a; M.8/86/10a; M.30/3/31a; <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Cha_Chi-tso\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Cha Chi-tso\">Cha Chi-tso</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>], <i>Tsui-wei lu</i>, 12\u4e0b/11a.]\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431519\">.mw-parser-output .wst-right{text-align:right}</style><div class=\"wst-right\" style=\"margin-right:2em;\">\n<p><span class=\"smallcaps\" style=\"font-variant:small-caps;\">George A. Kennedy</span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<p><br />\n&#32;\n</p>\n</div>\n</div>"},"links":[{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Cha Chi-tso"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Maci"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Mandahai"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Sun Ch'\u00eang-tsung"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Tsu Ta-shou"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Yang Hao"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Y\u00fcan Ch'ung-huan"},{"ns":102,"exists":"","*":"Author:Arthur W. Hummel"},{"ns":102,"exists":"","*":"Author:George A. Kennedy"}],"sections":[],"wikitext":{"*":"{{Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period\n | volume   = 1\n | onlysection = man kuei\n | previous = Maci\n | next     = Mandahai\n | from     = 573\n | to       = 574\n | notes    =\n | section_author = George A. Kennedy\n}}{{DEFAULTSORT:Man Kuei}}"}}}